WY.)  There being no comments or input and purpose of the hearing having been met, Mayor
Spiker closed the hearing.  The item was referred to Finance Committee.
A transfer of ownership for a retail liquor license application filed with the City Clerk’s
Office for Bedrock Seafood, Inc., d/b/a River Mountain Seafood Restaurant, 112 W. 17 St.,
th
Cheyenne, WY. (License currently held by JKLL, Inc., d/b/a Game On Sports Bar, 112 W. 17th
St., Cheyenne, WY.)  John Swatzel, Vice President of Bedrock Seafood, Inc., applicant,
introduced himself and explained the positive reception he is receiving from staff and the
community relative to the proposed new business operation.  There being no further comments
or input and purpose of the hearing having been met, Mayor Spiker closed the hearing.  The item
was referred to Finance Committee.
A transfer of location for a retail liquor license application filed with the City Clerk’s
Office for HEJE, Inc., [d/b/a to be determined], 4503 Western Way, Cheyenne, WY. (License
currently located at 3839 E. Lincolnway, Cheyenne, WY.)  Upon inquiry regarding the new site
location proposed for license operations, and anticipated use under the license, Carol Intlekofer,
City Clerk, advised the retail liquor license is proposed to be transferred from its currently
“parked” site known as the former Outlaw Saloon on E. Lincolnway to 4503 Western Way, and
operations under the license will be for a package store only (no lounge) without a drive-up
window.  Ms. Broyles submitted copies of a map depicting the proposed new location for the
license.  Comment followed regarding Ward III residents’ concerns with placement of a package
liquor store establishment in the area, and it was noted a grant was being sought to provide more
law enforcement presence in the vicinity.  John Rogers, attorney speaking on behalf of HEJE,
Inc., applicant, submitted a copy of the written decision from the Board of Adjustment allowing
zoning approval for the placement of the package store at the new site.  There being no further
comments or input and purpose of the hearing having been met, Mayor Spiker closed the
hearing.  The item was referred to Finance Committee.
“AN ORDINANCE AMENDING SECTION 6.12.020, RABIES TAGS, OF CHAPTER
6.12, DOG AND CAT REGISTRATION, AND SECTION 6.16.020, RABIES VACCINATION
REQUIRED, OF CHAPTER 6.16, RABIES CONTROL, OF TITLE 6, ANIMALS, OF THE
CODE OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE, WYOMING.”  John Fafoutakis spoke in support of the
ordinance.  Reporting for Public Services Committee, Mr. Segrave moved to approve on third
and final reading, seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Mr. Segrave moved to amend by substitute,
seconded by Mr. Pierson.  It was advised the substitute addresses registration concerns. Karon
Volk spoke in favor of the substitute.  Upon inquiry, Bob Mason, City Staff Attorney, clarified
that language contained in Section 6.12.020(A), referring to animal registration for
Cheyenne/Laramie County, meant that registration acquired through local veterinary offices will
pertain to both city and county residents.  Discussion topics included: County Board of Health’s
approval of the rabies vaccination being administered every 3 years as an option; current
ordinance history; input on proposed ordinance changes from local veterinarians, dog breeders
and the Director of the Cheyenne Animal Control/Shelter; rabies vaccination, registration and
regulation; due diligence efforts to provide notification of proposed changes to the community;
concern with potential rabies outbreak in the community, and purpose of the ordinance.  John
Rogers expressed support for the ordinance change, and indicated his belief that, due to local
media coverage, adequate publicity on the proposed ordinance had been provided. Mr.
Fafoutakis commented in support of the proposed substitute, and Ms. Volk advised the proposed
ordinance doesn’t change reporting or animal registration requirements.  Further discussion
```

### Page 3 of 8

```text
continued on pet owners’ requirement to update information for yearly registration with their
veterinarian’s office, and language pertaining to Section 6.12.020(A) previously referenced, with
further information provided by Attorney Mason.  Mr. Brown moved to amend the substitute by
striking the words “Cheyenne/Laramie County” from Section 6.12.020(A), and inserting “City of
Cheyenne”, seconded by Mr. Collins.  Upon inquiry, Acting City Attorney Bob Nicholas stated
the inclusion of Laramie County was more for informational purposes, not a mandatory
requirement, and, during discussion, Attorney Mason provided further information relative to the
reference to Laramie County.  Discussion followed on the proposed amendment.  It was advised
that currently the County provides pet owners the option of three year vs. annual vaccinations.
Motion to amend failed (due to a tie vote).  Voting “yes” – Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Mr. Collins,
Dr. Rinne and Mr. Segrave.  Voting “no” – Ms. Case, Mr. Laybourn, Mr. Pierson, Mr. Valdez
and Mayor Spiker. Discussion continued on the proposed substitute and included: Purpose of the
ordinance; review committee composition and involvement during ordinance compilation; public
representation; involvement by local veterinarians, and content areas of the ordinance applicable
to vaccinations, reporting and registration.  Upon inquiry,  Mr. Mason provided vaccination and
registration information.  Motion to amend by substitute carried.  Voting “yes” - all members of
the governing body with the exception of Ms. Case voting “no”.  Discussion continued on the
main motion as amended and included: Vaccination certification time frames; yearly registration
requirement and any affiliated fees that may be assessed by veterinarians; Animal Shelter’s
involvement with registration, and concerns with wild animals within the City that may be rabies
carriers.  Mr. Mason explained information contained on rabies/registration animal tags as issued
by local veterinarians.  Main motion as amended by substitute carried. Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body with the exception of Ms. Case and Mr. Laybourn voting “no”.
